course: dictionary
lesson: 7890
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which one the writer means. The word rank is a good example. In «Male kangaroos box to win rank», rank means a position in a group, higher or lower than others.
explain: Look at the job the word does. In «Rank the numbers from smallest to largest», rank is an action: it means to put things in order by size. In «That bin is rank!», the bin tells you rank is an informal word meaning very smelly.
explain: Always test your meaning by putting it back into the sentence. If «a row of people» does not fit in «That bin is rank», try a different meaning until one makes sense.
fact: Rank can be a noun, a verb or an adjective, so its job in the sentence matters.
fact: As a noun, rank means a position in a group or list, or an official level in the army or police.
fact: As a verb, to rank means to put things in order by size, score or importance.
fact: As a noun, rank can also mean a row or line of people or things, as in «ranks of soldiers» or «a taxi rank».
fact: In UK playground slang, «rank» means very smelly or nasty. As an adjective it can also mean complete or total, as in «a rank beginner».
exercise: «Rank the numbers from smallest to largest.» What does rank mean here? | Very smelly | A row of soldiers standing still | An official level in the police | *To put things in order by size
exercise: «Male kangaroos box to win rank.» What does rank mean here? | Complete or total | *A position in a group, higher or lower than others | A row of people waiting | Very smelly or nasty
exercise: «That bin is rank!» What does rank mean here? | A place where taxis wait | Complete or total | *Very smelly or nasty | A position in a list
test: «She is a rank beginner at chess.» What does rank mean here? | To put the players in order | A row of chess pieces | *Complete or total | Very smelly
test: «Ranks of soldiers marched past the castle.» What does ranks mean here? | To put in order by size | *Rows or lines of people | Very smelly or nasty | A position in a list
exam: «Voters rank candidates, and each area elects several people.» What does rank mean here? | Very nasty | Complete or total | A row of seats in a hall | *Put in order of preference
